# No image is built from this directory. The agent image GoReleaser publishes
# comes from Dockerfile.agent, FROM scratch and one COPY of a prebuilt
# ${TARGETPLATFORM}/mikroscope-agent, and dockers_v2 builds it in a context
# GoReleaser assembles itself: a temporary directory holding that Dockerfile
# and each platform's binary, and nothing from the working tree (GoReleaser
# v2.18.1, internal/pipe/docker/v2). The side-loadable agent tars are written
# without Docker at all, by internal/image. So this file is read only by a
# build pointed at the root, `docker build -f Dockerfile.agent .` by hand or a
# Dockerfile added later. Without it that context is the whole working tree,
# 489 MB measured on 2026-09-15; with it, 1.65 MB, of which a build from source
# would read go.mod, go.sum, version.go with the VERSION file it embeds, cmd/
# and internal/, 1.58 MB.
#
# Deliberately a denylist. An allowlist would be tighter, but it silently drops
# whatever is added at the root later that a build does need, and a build from
# source does read the root.
#
# The denylist of ghchronicle's .dockerignore, the owner's decision, adapted to
# this tree. It keeps out .env and *.token, so a router's API password, an
# agent's bearer token or a sink credential never reaches a layer of an image,
# and site/, the host's own binaries and the scanner's working directory, so
# the context stays near the megabyte and a half a build reads instead of
# hundreds of them.

# Working notes: not part of the published tool.
plan/

# History and CI definitions: a build reads neither, and .git alone is 26 MB of
# the tree.
.git
.github

# Local configuration and credentials must never reach an image layer. `.env`
# holds the router's address and API user password, the agent token and the
# InfluxDB and Grafana tokens; `.env.*` is the variant a second environment gets
# (.env.local, .env.production), and it holds the same kind of secret as the
# file it copies. .claude/ is a coding agent's local settings for this
# checkout.
.env
.env.*
*.token
.claude/

# Build output from the host: bin/ is `make build` and `make build-agent-all`,
# build/ is the agent image tars `make agent-tars` and `make agent-smoke`
# write, dist/ is GoReleaser, and the loose files are what `go build
# ./cmd/<name>`, `go test` and `make cover-check` leave in the working
# directory: coverage.out and coverage.html. The three command names are
# spelled out because none of them carries an extension, and an image build
# would upload them and then ignore them. *.tar is an agent image tar saved at
# the root, and captures/ and lab/ are where device captures and disk images
# land, as .gitignore has them. golangci-report*.txt is a lint run saved to a
# file for reading later, which a build has no use for either.
#
# Unlike .gitignore, a pattern here is matched against the whole path, so
# "mikroscope" names the file at the root and cannot reach cmd/mikroscope.
bin/
build/
dist/
gen_brand
mikroscope
mikroscope-agent
*.exe
*.test
*.out
*.tar
coverage*
captures/
lab/
golangci-report*.txt

# The scanner's configuration, and the working directory `make sonar` leaves at
# the root: sonar-project.properties sets no sonar.working.directory, so
# sonar-scanner uses its default. A build reads neither.
sonar-project.properties
.scannerwork/

# The documentation site: node_modules alone is 358 MB, and nothing in an image
# reads a line of it. This one entry is most of the difference between the
# 489 MB context and the 1.65 MB one.
site/

# Prose, images and generated dashboards. None of it is embedded: the module's
# only go:embed is in version.go, which reads the VERSION file beside it at the
# root, and neither of those two is excluded here. So copying these in would
# only invalidate the compile layer every time a document is edited.
#
# Before excluding anything else at the root, check it is not what version.go
# embeds: a build that cannot see VERSION fails to compile, because a go:embed
# pattern that matches no file is an error.
docs/
brand/
dashboards/
*.md

# The end-to-end suite builds both binaries itself and drives them against a
# fake agent and recorders that stand in for the sinks, and testdata/ is the
# reference device's captured /proc that the unit tests read. A build never
# compiles the one or reads the other.
test/
testdata/
